cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Twenty Connected-Device Projects on an RTOS and Embedded Linux"
message: "If you refer to this work, please cite it as below."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Pagaran
    given-names: Joseph Ambrose
    orcid: ""
abstract: >-
  Twenty connected-device projects built around four behaviours rather than
  around a list of technologies: presence with a hold and a release, a sample
  that is allowed to leave the device, a signed image that can roll back, and a
  radio that sleeps. Each behaviour is built in full by exactly one chapter and
  cited by the rest. Fifteen chapters run a real-time operating system on a
  Cortex-M7 or Cortex-M33 microcontroller, four run Linux on single-board
  computers, and one spans both deliberately, because a device lifecycle is the
  one subject that cannot be shown on either alone. Covers devicetree overlays
  and an out-of-tree driver, the measured cost of kernel primitives, settings
  that survive a power cut, a signed image with confirm and revert, a fleet
  update and its shadow, a duty-cycled radio, device identity with enrolment,
  rotation and revocation, the same application moved to a second processor
  family, hardware-in-the-loop testing on every push, and a build system
  exercised through its extensible software development kit. Every measurement
  names its instrument or reads not measured; outlines are solid for hardware on
  the bench, dashed for a model standing in and dotted for hardware that is
  absent with the cost of its absence written down. Nothing claims a shipped
  product on this operating system, a battery life in years, or radar.
keywords:
  - embedded systems
  - real-time operating system
  - embedded Linux
  - devicetree
  - firmware update
  - low power
  - CAN
  - device identity
license: MIT
